  "strongest_attack": "Strongest attack attempted (zero-slack endpoint, single point of failure): the entire theorem rests on one tight inequality, n-1 = 7 = c(5), with no margin, while every other step in the peel carries strict slack (24 > vcd(M_{5,7}) = 23; base degree 16 > vcd(M_5) = 15; and the pigeonhole |i| >= 9 on 8 slots forcing some i_s = 2). Worse, this tightness is structural rather than accidental: the RED-0001 residual condition 2d-16 = vcd is equivalent to 2g+n = 18, i.e. n-1 = 17-2g, and the published table has c(g) = 17-2g precisely for g = 5, 6, 7 (and c(3) = 11 = 17-6). So the CKgP hypothesis is met at equality for (3,12), (5,8), (6,6) and (7,4) alike, and any correction that made the published CKgP range strict, or that lowered c(5) from 7 to 6, would collapse this proof with no fallback -- and would simultaneously collapse FND-0195's own (6,6) conclusion. I could not make this attack land against the packet as bound: FND-0195 is corroborated, states the range with an inclusive '<=', and itself exercises the endpoint at n-1 = 5 = c(6); ATT-0142 therefore uses the endpoint exactly as the adjudicated finding licenses it, and re-litigating c(5) would be re-opening a recorded source bound, not finding a defect in this artifact.\n\nSecondary attacks, all of which failed to land. (1) Transport of a (6,6)-verified mechanism to (5,8): I checked that FND-0196's disposal (3) base case holds for |j| <= k - vcd(M_g) - 1, which equals n identically for k = vcd(M_{g,n}), so the recorded '|i| <= 6, 20 > 19' instance is a specialization of a (g,n)-uniform bound and the '|i| <= 8, 16 > 15' form is correct, not a rescaled guess; likewise the pigeonhole step is uniform. (2) Poincare/twist attack: I re-derived H^{BM}_{16} = H^{24}(20), verified that weight -16 maps to weight 24 under the -2d = -40 shift, that both named groups are the lowest-weight pieces in their degrees, and that Q(-12)(20) = Q(8) has weight -16; nothing is off by a twist or by one unit of weight, and the FND-0020 'mixed R^1, off by one unit of weight' failure mode is absent because no punctured-curve local system is used. (3) Wrong-fibration attack: the Kunneth computation could have been run on the non-proper f: M_{g,n} -> M_g, where the FND-0197-excluded identification and the FND-0037 extension effect would bite; the attempt instead uses the proper C^8 over M_5 and states this, so the obstruction is genuinely avoided rather than renamed. (4) Semisimplification-versus-isomorphism: FND-0033 is nominally scoped to (6,6), but the step is non-load-bearing because FND-0195 invokes the same fixed-weight semisimplicity internally and the group is polarizable pure by Deligne's compactification description. (5) Forbidden at-vcd inference: 24 = vcd(M_{5,8}) is never used to vanish anything; the two vanishings live on M_{5,7} and M_5. (6) Motive-status attack: CKgP Prop 4.5 gives 'algebraic of Tate type' and the proof consumes only 'Tate type', so the flow is strong-to-weak and no predicted motive or tautological-generation claim is smuggled in. (7) Scope attacks that do not apply to this lemma at all: no coarse moduli, no integral coefficients, no Gysin or codimension shift, no boundary stratum, no Aut(Gamma) invariants-versus-coinvariants or determinant-edge convention, no spectral-sequence differential or degeneration claim, and no stable-pair/evaluation-failure geometry (FND-0050/FND-0052 target a different, (6,6)-specific route and are not obligations for this peel). (8) Provenance: ATT-0142 is a revision-migration recovery authored against packet v1, so its citations could in principle have been written against different finding texts; I checked each of FND-0001, FND-0033, FND-0035, FND-0195, FND-0196, FND-0197 against the v2 packet bound by this task and each is present with content that supports the use made of it, and the task packet_sha256 a898256a..., packet_binding_sha256 6a765b58..., campaign_revision 2, context_revision 2, subproblem C58-OPEN and the target dictionary all match the artifact after the recorded HARNESS-IDENTITY-COERCE-0001 normalizations.\n\nConclusion: the artifact is a correct and minimal specialization of a corroborated general finding, and the theorem it states -- the exact open group only, with no compact-moduli and no boundary-image consequence -- is proved. Confirming this lemma does not verify the (5,8) case of the campaign nor the C66BAR boundary-image theorem, and the C58-BOUNDARY, C58-COMPACT, C66BAR-SEP and C66BAR-IMAGE nodes remain open.",
  "subproblem_id": "C58-OPEN",
  "target": {
    "chow_codimension": 12,
    "compact_cohomology_degree": 16,
    "compact_tate_type": "Q(-8)",
    "dimension": 20,
    "g": 5,
    "n": 8,
    "open_bm_degree": 16,
    "open_bm_tate_type": "Q(8)",
    "open_bm_weight": -16,
    "ordinary_cohomology_degree": 24,
    "ordinary_tate_type": "Q(-12)",
    "ordinary_weight": 24,
    "poincare_twist": 20
  },
  "theorem_statement": "The exact group W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{5,8};Q), equivalently W_{24}H^{24}(M_{5,8};Q)(20), is a direct sum of Q(8); a disproof must exhibit a surviving non-Tate summand in this exact group. This theorem does not conclude H^{16}(Mbar_{5,8};Q) and does not conclude the campaign boundary-image theorem.",
  "verdict": "confirmed"
}

